1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,440
Two blue solutions.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:04,240
Your eye can tell which one is darker,

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:06,800
but not how much copper is in either.

4
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:10,400
A calibration turns that colour into a number.

5
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,640
You measure how much light some solutions of

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,160
known concentration absorb, draw the line they make,

7
00:00:16,281 --> 00:00:18,400
then read a sample you don't know straight

8
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,760
off that line.

9
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:22,000
Today you build one for copper, and use

10
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,000
it on rinse water from a copper plating

11
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:24,640
workshop.

12
00:00:25,867 --> 00:00:27,946
The aim is to find the concentration of

13
00:00:28,107 --> 00:00:30,346
copper ions in that rinse water.

14
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:33,626
Each solution whose concentration you know is called

15
00:00:33,707 --> 00:00:34,706
a standard.

16
00:00:34,987 --> 00:00:38,106
The independent variable is the standards' concentration.

17
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:41,706
The dependent variable is their absorbance, how much

18
00:00:41,787 --> 00:00:43,226
light each one soaks up.

19
00:00:43,707 --> 00:00:45,466
Everything else stays the same.

20
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:49,386
Start from a stock solution of copper sulfate,

21
00:00:49,467 --> 00:00:51,946
point two zero zero moles per litre.

22
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:54,986
Fill one burette with stock and another with

23
00:00:55,147 --> 00:00:57,786
distilled water, rinsing each with the liquid it

24
00:00:57,867 --> 00:00:58,786
will hold.

25
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:01,546
Run one millilitre of stock and nine of

26
00:01:01,627 --> 00:01:03,626
water into a tube, and mix.

27
00:01:04,107 --> 00:01:07,306
That's a tenfold dilution, so point zero two

28
00:01:07,387 --> 00:01:08,666
zero moles per litre.

29
00:01:09,307 --> 00:01:12,586
Then use two, three, four and six millilitres

30
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:14,986
of stock, each topped up with water to

31
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:16,026
ten millilitres.

32
00:01:16,507 --> 00:01:19,066
The strongest is point one two zero.

33
00:01:20,267 --> 00:01:20,986
Which light?

34
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:23,466
A blue solution absorbs at the red end

35
00:01:23,547 --> 00:01:24,266
of the spectrum.

36
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:27,146
A scan shows its biggest absorbance near eight

37
00:01:27,227 --> 00:01:30,746
hundred nanometres, in the near infrared, so set

38
00:01:30,827 --> 00:01:32,346
this spectrophotometer there.

39
00:01:32,747 --> 00:01:35,066
That's where each small change in copper gives

40
00:01:35,147 --> 00:01:37,066
the biggest change in absorbance.

41
00:01:37,307 --> 00:01:40,106
A school colourimeter can't, so use its red

42
00:01:40,187 --> 00:01:42,146
filter, with its own calibration.

43
00:01:42,347 --> 00:01:44,746
Then zero it with a blank, a cuvette

44
00:01:44,787 --> 00:01:46,386
of the same distilled water.

45
00:01:46,587 --> 00:01:48,426
The glass and the water absorb a little

46
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:51,226
too, and the blank takes that away, so

47
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:53,306
what's left comes from copper.

48
00:01:54,534 --> 00:01:57,253
Hold each cuvette by its ridged sides, and

49
00:01:57,334 --> 00:02:00,293
wipe the clear sides, because a fingerprint scatters

50
00:02:00,374 --> 00:02:02,133
light and adds absorbance.

51
00:02:02,454 --> 00:02:04,693
Rinse the cuvette with the next standard before

52
00:02:04,774 --> 00:02:06,693
you fill it, so the last one doesn't

53
00:02:06,734 --> 00:02:07,653
dilute it.

54
00:02:07,974 --> 00:02:09,973
Work from the most dilute to the most

55
00:02:10,054 --> 00:02:11,013
concentrated.

56
00:02:12,234 --> 00:02:13,273
Here are the readings.

57
00:02:13,834 --> 00:02:17,033
Plot absorbance up the side and concentration along

58
00:02:17,074 --> 00:02:19,193
the bottom, with the blank as a point

59
00:02:19,274 --> 00:02:20,313
at zero.

60
00:02:20,394 --> 00:02:22,393
The blank and the first four standards sit

61
00:02:22,474 --> 00:02:24,686
close to one straight line.

62
00:02:24,741 --> 00:02:26,953
The fifth falls well below it.

63
00:02:27,034 --> 00:02:29,553
At high absorbance, so little light reaches the

64
00:02:29,594 --> 00:02:32,553
detector that the reading is less trustworthy.

65
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:34,553
So this line only holds up to point

66
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:37,193
zero eight zero moles per litre.

67
00:02:37,274 --> 00:02:38,873
That's its linear range.

68
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,420
Now the rinse water.

69
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:44,820
It reads one point five two, far above

70
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:46,460
the top of the linear range.

71
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:48,660
You could extend the line, but the fifth

72
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:50,900
standard has just shown that the line bends

73
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:52,820
up there, so don't trust it.

74
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:54,700
Dilute it instead.

75
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:57,460
Five millilitres of rinse water and five of

76
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:00,180
distilled water halve its concentration.

77
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:02,500
Three readings now give a mean of point

78
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:05,140
eight four five, inside the range.

79
00:03:06,367 --> 00:03:08,606
Read across from point eight four five to

80
00:03:08,647 --> 00:03:12,046
the line, then down: point zero seven one

81
00:03:12,207 --> 00:03:13,886
zero moles per litre.

82
00:03:14,207 --> 00:03:15,966
That's the diluted solution, though.

83
00:03:16,287 --> 00:03:18,286
You halved it, so the rinse water is

84
00:03:18,447 --> 00:03:21,246
double that, point one four two moles per

85
00:03:21,327 --> 00:03:21,886
litre.

86
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:23,820
Reliable?

87
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,780
The three readings agree within about point zero

88
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,580
zero six, and a second dilution gives the

89
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:30,460
same answer.

90
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:31,780
Accurate?

91
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:33,740
Your teacher made a check standard from a

92
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:37,340
separate weighing, point zero five zero zero moles

93
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:38,300
per litre.

94
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:40,700
Read from your line, it comes out at

95
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:43,660
point zero four nine seven, within about one

96
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:45,340
percent of its true value.

97
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:47,286
Valid?

98
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:50,086
The method measures copper only if copper is

99
00:03:50,207 --> 00:03:52,726
the only thing absorbing at that wavelength.

100
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:56,086
A cloudy sample scatters light and reads high,

101
00:03:56,167 --> 00:03:57,446
so filter it first.

102
00:03:58,087 --> 00:04:01,446
Filtering can't remove dissolved substances, so your teacher

103
00:04:01,527 --> 00:04:04,086
has checked this sample for other absorbers.

104
00:04:04,407 --> 00:04:06,726
Then read it at the same wavelength, in

105
00:04:06,807 --> 00:04:09,286
the same cuvette, with the same blank, and

106
00:04:09,447 --> 00:04:11,286
inside the linear range.

107
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:13,606
Only then does the line apply to it.

108
00:04:14,834 --> 00:04:16,153
Now the risks.

109
00:04:16,434 --> 00:04:19,873
Copper sulfate is harmful if swallowed, irritates eyes

110
00:04:19,954 --> 00:04:22,593
and skin, and is very toxic to aquatic

111
00:04:22,794 --> 00:04:23,073
life.

112
00:04:23,634 --> 00:04:26,833
Wear gloves and glasses, and every copper solution

113
00:04:26,914 --> 00:04:29,793
goes into heavy-metal waste, never the sink.

114
00:04:30,354 --> 00:04:32,593
Treat the rinse water the same way, because

115
00:04:32,674 --> 00:04:34,833
it may hold acid or other metals.

116
00:04:35,554 --> 00:04:38,033
Fill the burettes below eye level, and fill

117
00:04:38,114 --> 00:04:40,913
cuvettes away from the instrument, so a spill

118
00:04:40,994 --> 00:04:42,593
can't reach its electronics.

119
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:45,000
Two lines to keep.

120
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,600
Read across, then down, and only inside the

121
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:49,320
standards.

122
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,200
And if you diluted it, multiply back by

123
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:53,480
the dilution factor.

124
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:55,340
Your turn.

125
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:58,780
A student's copper standards give the calibration line

126
00:04:58,860 --> 00:04:59,500
on screen.

127
00:05:00,060 --> 00:05:02,940
Ten millilitres of an unknown copper solution is

128
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,140
diluted to twenty five millilitres, and the diluted

129
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:09,340
solution reads point three two five.

130
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:12,300
Calculate the copper ion concentration in the original

131
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,220
solution.

132
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:14,860
Pause here to answer.

133
00:05:25,267 --> 00:05:27,906
Read across from point three two five, then

134
00:05:27,987 --> 00:05:31,826
down: point two five zero moles per litre.

135
00:05:32,067 --> 00:05:33,586
That's the diluted solution.

136
00:05:34,067 --> 00:05:36,546
Twenty five over ten is a dilution factor

137
00:05:36,627 --> 00:05:39,346
of two point five, so the original is

138
00:05:39,507 --> 00:05:41,946
point six two five moles per litre.

139
00:05:42,227 --> 00:05:44,466
The trap is stopping at point two five

140
00:05:44,547 --> 00:05:45,106
zero.

141
00:05:46,334 --> 00:05:46,973
Last one.

142
00:05:47,534 --> 00:05:49,693
A student measures the standards in a clean

143
00:05:49,774 --> 00:05:52,893
cuvette, then measures the unknown in a cuvette

144
00:05:52,974 --> 00:05:55,533
with a fingerprint on one clear side.

145
00:05:55,854 --> 00:05:59,213
Explain how this affects the calculated concentration, and

146
00:05:59,334 --> 00:06:00,813
propose an improvement.

147
00:06:01,134 --> 00:06:02,413
Pause here to answer.

148
00:06:12,834 --> 00:06:15,953
The fingerprint scatters some light, so less reaches

149
00:06:15,994 --> 00:06:18,953
the detector, and the absorbance reads high.

150
00:06:19,234 --> 00:06:21,873
A higher absorbance, read from the line, gives

151
00:06:21,954 --> 00:06:24,353
a concentration that's too high.

152
00:06:24,434 --> 00:06:26,673
Fix it by holding cuvettes by their ridged

153
00:06:26,754 --> 00:06:29,633
sides and wiping the clear sides before every

154
00:06:29,714 --> 00:06:30,753
reading.

155
00:06:30,794 --> 00:06:33,233
The trap is averaging three readings.

156
00:06:33,354 --> 00:06:35,713
Each one still has the fingerprint, so they're

157
00:06:35,834 --> 00:06:36,993
all high together.

158
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:40,200
So, to trust a calibration:

159
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:44,040
make your standards by careful dilution, zero with

160
00:06:44,067 --> 00:06:46,680
a blank, and keep the unknown inside the

161
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:49,640
linear range, diluting it if you must.

162
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:52,520
Then check accuracy with a standard you already

163
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:53,320
know.

164
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:57,640
Next: calibration and water-analysis data practice.

165
00:06:58,867 --> 00:07:00,306
Here are the key notes.

166
00:07:00,547 --> 00:07:02,466
Pause the video if you need more time

167
00:07:02,547 --> 00:07:03,346
to copy them.
